WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:07.175
Three corporate conglomerates control approximately eighty percent of Canadian television revenue: Bell, Rogers, and Corus-Shaw.

00:00:07.125 --> 00:00:11.579
Postmedia owns approximately one hundred and thirty newspaper brands.

00:00:11.579 --> 00:00:16.340
Two hundred and fifty-one local media outlets have closed since 2008.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:25.534
The Rogers-Shaw merger — valued at twenty-six billion dollars — was approved in 2023, further consolidating control over content and distribution.

00:00:25.534 --> 00:00:32.874
The CBC receives one point four billion dollars in annual public funding and commands approximately five percent of the audience.

00:00:32.874 --> 00:00:37.874
An additional five hundred and ninety-five million dollars flows through media subsidies.

00:00:37.874 --> 00:00:44.806
These same conglomerates own the television networks, the radio stations, the newspapers, and the internet service providers.

00:00:44.806 --> 00:00:48.034
They control the content and the delivery mechanism.

00:00:48.034 --> 00:00:53.784
When three companies own the news and the pipes it travels through, the Ministry of Truth is not a metaphor.

00:00:53.784 --> 00:00:55.727
It is a business model.
